{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/the-world-below/1555881-grimdark-magazine-s-review-of-the-world-below","id":1555881,"title":"Grimdark Magazine's review of The World Below","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Earthbane Cycle","The World Below"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-26T01:39:41-08:00","id":1555881,"title":"Grimdark Magazine's review of The World Below","content":"<br /> <a href=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below.webp\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"The World Below Cover Image\" width=\"232\" height=\"300\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-thumb-url=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/review-the-world-below-by-matthew-dawkins/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/rev...tthew-dawkins/</a><br /> <br /> <i>The World Below </i>by Matthew Dawkins, Diana DiMicco, Chant Evans, Kim Godwin, Ed Greenwood, Danielle Lauzon, Travis Legge, Michele Masala, and Leath Sheales is a self-contained dark fantasy tabletop roleplaying game. The premise is many races inhabit a vast underground setting (the titular World Below) and they must fight for survival in a complicated network of caverns that are empowered by a mysterious magic source called the Well.<br /> <br /> I\u2019m a huge fan of the Underdark concept. First created by Gary Gygax in the legendary <i>Against the Giants</i> modules before eventually leading to the Drow series, it has its roots on many older concepts. Things like Edgar Rice Burrough\u2019s <i>Pellucidar</i>, Jules Verne\u2019s <i>Journey to the Center of the Earth</i>, Tolkien\u2019s Mines of Moria, and the chthonic realms of Greek Mythology. Even the conspiratorial concept of the Hollow Earth. More recently, R.A. Salvatore, Elaine Cunningham, and other authors expanded on the Underdark in their books with the idea of vast underground kingdoms ruled by gnomes or drow.<br /> <br /> However, the Underdark and Hollow Earth concepts have never quite reached their full potential gaming wise (with rare exceptions like Mystara\u2019s <i>Hollow World</i> in 1990). Most use the world below for a short incursion and never really develop the ideas of a vibrant interrelated ecosystem set underground. How do people survive in such a place? On what? How do such cultures interact when they are forced by environment to compete for basic resources?<br /> <br /> <i>The World Below </i>introduces us to a world where the population of the surface has been forced underground for reasons they do not remember (in one of the setting\u2019s great mysteries). A variety of unique fantasy races and humans are forced to struggle with one another as well as the local species for the caverns that are habitable. The mysterious energies in the World Below (called \u201cKaos\u201d) do not leave their dwellers unaffected and a variety of strange magical powers are detailed for them to use and be changed by. Kaos energy may be your friend or foe depending on how you channel it and the game offers many special abilities as well as curses to draw from.<br /> <br /> <i>The World Below</i>\u2018s authors take special care in providing a lived-in experience for gamers so that it\u2019s unique to this setting versus another reiteration of generic D&amp;D. I knew the game had me when it provided a wholly original set of profanity for gamers to use in-game. That sort of thing was what I loved in <i>Shadowrun</i>. It also provides a very clear sense of \u201cwhat do you DO in this place?\u201d that is often a failing of new fantasy settings. Adventurers work for guilds and settlements to find the supplies necessary to survive as well as thrive in an environment that is habitable but not necessarily hospitable. It also establishes antagonists ranging from low level monstrous hordes to the mysterious Well Liches.<br /> <br /> I like the Storypath Ultra system: the abilities, enemies, conditions, complications, the magic system (structured and Kaos freeform magic), downtime, community building, etc. It strikes a nice balance between the mechanical nature of D&amp;D and overly freeform modern RPGs. Combat is lethal but not excessively so and rewards planning as well as intelligent use of your abilities over chipping away at health. Which is to say, \u201cThere\u2019s a lot less math here but enough to get by.\u201d<br /> <br /> A lot of care was taken to figuring out this world works and how the various parties in the place think of one another. There\u2019s care to religion, festivals, and economics that you don\u2019t ordinarily get out of settings aside from, say, Westeros. There\u2019s no alignment system and virtually everyone has a chip on their shoulder about someone else. That\u2019s not to say the conflicts aren\u2019t epic and morality tends to go to the wayside when one group wants to eliminate you for taking \u201ctheir\u201d caverns. It\u2019s very Sword and Sorcery in its ethics and I happen to like that. Survival and coin are the goals with it a bonus if you save your community (at least how I read it).<br /> <br /> Did I like <i>The World Below</i>? Absolutely. I enjoyed it from two perspectives. First I enjoyed it as a long time fantasy reader and appreciating it from the perspective of someone who likes reading about settings. I own many books that I never actually get to run and it\u2019s nice to see the authors run wild with their design. Second of all, I actually do think this is the kind of setting that I will run as I love the idea of competing groups of adventurers fighting over a vast underground hellscape like a <i>Mad Max</i> underground.<br /> <br /> ----<br /> <br /> Yeah, I got a chance to review this game for GDM and I really enjoyed it. It's the kind of dark fantasy world-building that focuses much more on the roleplaying element than the dice or combat and I think it's all the better for it. <br /> <br /> It certainly makes the underground a lot more viable as a basis for a campaign than anything I've seen so far.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":12002,"user_link":"http